\b WASLE Revised Report Sections\b0\par
\par
\b Abstract\b0\par
Last-mile delivery is one of the most complex and costly segments of modern logistics, accounting for a significant share of total supply chain costs worldwide. In Lebanon, this challenge is further intensified by fragmented infrastructure, the absence of formal addressing systems, a largely cash-based economy, and the lack of integrated platforms capable of coordinating the multiple actors involved in delivery operations.\par
\par
This project presents WASLE, a smart pickup-point delivery coordination platform designed to address these challenges by supporting five key user roles: customers, merchants, drivers, delivery companies, and pickup point operators within a unified digital platform. The objective is to provide a secure, scalable, and efficient alternative to traditional home delivery models while improving logistics coordination in underserved areas.\par
\par
The system was developed using Flutter for the cross-platform frontend and Supabase for the backend, integrating database management, OTP-based authentication, real-time synchronization, and server-side business logic through Edge Functions. In addition to role-based access control and backend constraints, the platform implements a smart automated order assignment system that assigns orders to eligible drivers based on delivery company affiliation, vehicle capacity constraints, and estimated route cost.\par
\par
To support accurate and reliable assignment decisions, the routing subsystem was implemented using a Supabase Edge Function integrated with the Google Distance Matrix API to estimate travel duration and distance. A database-backed caching mechanism was introduced to reduce redundant API calls and improve performance, while a Haversine-based fallback strategy ensures service continuity when external routing services are unavailable.\par
\par
The platform was validated through both functional and performance-oriented testing. The evaluation confirmed correct same-company filtering, capacity-aware driver selection, route-based optimization, fallback behavior, and reliable route persistence. Iterative refinements also addressed reliability and efficiency issues encountered during development, including query behavior and upload-related bottlenecks.\par
\par
The final system is production-ready and demonstrates strong potential to reduce delivery costs, improve assignment efficiency, and enhance the scalability of last-mile logistics operations. More broadly, WASLE highlights how well-designed digital platforms can address complex delivery challenges while creating meaningful economic and societal impact.\par
