\b 1 Introduction\b0\par
Last-mile delivery, the final segment of the supply chain connecting a distribution hub to the end customer, is widely recognized as one of the most operationally complex and costly phases of the logistics process. This complexity arises from several factors, including the high variability of delivery destinations, the difficulty of coordinating multiple independent actors, frequent failed delivery attempts that require re-delivery, and the absence of integrated digital platforms capable of managing stakeholder interactions within a unified workflow.\par
\par
In Lebanon, these global challenges are further intensified by country-specific structural constraints. These include fragmented road infrastructure, the absence of a formal national addressing system, a predominantly cash-based economy, and a logistics ecosystem composed largely of small, independent delivery companies operating without formal dispatch systems. As a result, the rapid growth of e-commerce continues to outpace the development of reliable delivery infrastructure, creating major operational inefficiencies for merchants, particularly small and medium-sized businesses.\par
\par
The complexity of the problem is further amplified by the involvement of multiple stakeholders in a typical delivery transaction. These include the merchant who creates the order, the delivery company responsible for dispatch, the driver executing the delivery, the customer expecting order visibility, and the pickup point operator acting as an intermediate collection node. In addition to physical delivery coordination, the process also involves payment handling and financial remittance, especially in cash-on-delivery scenarios. Coordinating these roles and workflows within a secure and consistent digital platform represents a complex engineering challenge with no single obvious solution.\par
\par
Beyond coordination alone, effective last-mile delivery also requires intelligent operational decision-making. Assigning the right order to the right driver depends not only on availability, but also on company affiliation, vehicle capacity, and route efficiency. This transforms the problem from a simple communication issue into a constrained logistics optimization problem involving multiple technical and business constraints.\par
\par
\b 1.1 Problem Identification\b0\par
Traditional logistics coordination in Lebanon relies on fragmented communication channels, primarily WhatsApp groups and phone calls, between merchants, dispatch centers, and drivers. This approach is error-prone, lacks traceability, and does not scale effectively. The absence of structured driver assignment workflows, route-aware dispatching, capacity-aware delivery validation, clear order lifecycle management, standardized pickup point procedures, and integrated payment reconciliation results in inefficient and opaque operations.\par
\par
Furthermore, cash-on-delivery transactions, which represent a major share of payments, are often handled manually, leading to financial inconsistencies and reconciliation issues. When pickup points are involved, the problem extends beyond parcel handover to include the secure transfer of collected funds back to the platform or merchant ecosystem. Without a formal remittance workflow, these transactions remain difficult to track, verify, and audit.\par
\par
These characteristics confirm that the WASLE problem is a complex engineering problem: it involves multiple interdependent stakeholders with partially conflicting requirements, lacks a straightforward solution, requires the integration of software engineering, logistics coordination, routing intelligence, and secure financial workflow design, and has significant economic, social, and operational consequences.\par
\par
\b 1.2 Project Objectives and Outcomes\b0\par
The main objective of the WASLE project is to design and implement a secure, scalable, and multi-role delivery coordination platform based on a pickup-point model.\par
\par
The specific objectives are as follows:\par
Develop a unified delivery coordination platform with interfaces for customers, merchants, drivers, delivery companies, and pickup point operators.\par
Implement a secure OTP-based authentication system with role-based access control and protected backend data access.\par
Design and deploy a smart automated order assignment system that enforces same-company assignment, vehicle-capacity constraints, and route-based driver selection.\par
Develop a structured pickup point workflow for parcel handling, cash collection, and remittance tracking.\par
Support both digital and cash-based payment flows, including online payment initiation, cash confirmation, and fallback collection mechanisms where digital remittance is unavailable.\par
Implement a routing subsystem capable of estimating travel duration and distance while maintaining reliability through caching and fallback estimation.\par
Validate system behavior through functional testing and performance-oriented evaluation.\par
\par
The expected outcomes include a fully functional prototype, secure data access mechanisms, efficient order lifecycle management, improved coordination between all stakeholders, more reliable and cost-efficient driver assignment decisions, and more transparent financial handling of cash-on-delivery transactions.\par
\par
