\b 2.2 Constraints\b0\par
\b 2.2.1 Technical Constraints\b0\par
The technical constraints define the measurable system limitations and implementation requirements necessary for the WASLE platform to operate efficiently, securely, and reliably within a multi-role distributed environment.\par
\par
Performance: The platform shall provide responsive API and database interactions under normal mobile network conditions, with particular attention to reducing latency in common operations such as order retrieval, authentication, automated assignment, route estimation, and payment-status updates.\par
\par
Operational Reliability: The automated order assignment workflow shall always return a valid system outcome, such as successful assignment, rejection due to business rules, or a descriptive failure message when assignment cannot be completed. Routing operations shall also return a valid result through either live API estimation, cached results, or fallback estimation.\par
\par
Security: Sensitive backend credentials, including privileged Supabase keys, Google Maps API credentials, and external payment-provider credentials, shall never be exposed to the client application. Privileged operations shall be restricted to trusted backend services and Edge Functions.\par
\par
Payment Integrity: Payment state transitions shall not be performed directly from the Flutter client. Instead, they shall be routed through controlled backend functions to preserve financial consistency and auditability.\par
\par
Authentication: JWT session tokens shall expire within 3,600 seconds (1 hour), with refresh token rotation enabled to reduce the risk of session hijacking.\par
\par
Compatibility: The Flutter application shall target commonly used Android and iOS mobile devices supported by the project configuration and Flutter toolchain.\par
\par
Database Integrity: Order lifecycle transitions, automated assignment decisions, route persistence, and payment-related state changes shall be validated against predefined business rules to ensure consistency and prevent invalid operations.\par
\par
Image Handling: Verification images submitted during pickup point application shall be uploaded in a format suitable for mobile networks and backend storage, while maintaining sufficient quality for administrative review.\par
\par
Capacity Enforcement: Automated assignment shall enforce vehicle-capacity constraints such that the current assigned load plus the new order demand does not exceed the driver's capacity limits in terms of weight, volume, and item count.\par
\par
Routing Cost Control: The routing subsystem shall minimize unnecessary external API calls by reusing cached route estimates whenever possible in order to control operational cost and improve scalability.\par
\par
\b 2.2.2 Non-technical Constraints\b0\par
In addition to technical limitations, the WASLE platform must satisfy several non-technical constraints related to usability, legal compliance, and operational feasibility.\par
\par
Accessibility and Usability: The user interface shall be intuitive and accessible to users with limited technical knowledge, following Material Design principles and mobile usability best practices.\par
\par
Legal Considerations: The system shall adopt GDPR principles as best practice, including data minimization and the ability for users to request deletion of their personal data.\par
\par
Safety and Trust: Drivers and pickup point applicants shall undergo verification or approval procedures before receiving access to operational platform features.\par
\par
Economic Accessibility: The platform shall require no specialized hardware and shall operate on standard consumer smartphones.\par
\par
Schedule: The Minimum Viable Product shall be designed, implemented, and tested within the academic semester timeline.\par
\par
Sustainability: Real-time updates shall rely primarily on event-driven synchronization mechanisms rather than continuous polling, reducing unnecessary network and energy consumption. In addition, route caching contributes to reduced external API usage and lower computational overhead.\par
\par
Maintainability: The codebase shall follow a modular, feature-based architecture with clear separation between data, business logic, and presentation layers.\par
\par
Constructability and physical safety constraints: These are not applicable, as the project is purely software-based.\par
\par
\b 2.2.3 Standards / Codes / Regulations / Policies\b0\par
The WASLE platform is designed in alignment with recognized standards and best practices intended to improve security, reliability, and usability.\par
\par
OWASP Mobile Security Testing Guide (MSTG): The project follows secure mobile development practices such as protecting secrets, avoiding insecure local storage of sensitive credentials, and using secure network communication.\par
\par
ISO/IEC 27001:2022: The system's authentication and access-control approach is conceptually aligned with information security management principles related to access restriction and secure handling of data.\par
\par
OAuth 2.0 / token-based authentication practices: Supabase authentication relies on token-based session management using JWTs, supporting secure authentication workflows.\par
\par
GDPR: The platform adopts GDPR-inspired principles such as data minimization and user-data protection as best practice.\par
\par
Material Design 3: The frontend follows Material-based design principles for usability, consistency, and accessibility.\par
\par
PostgreSQL access-control mechanisms: The backend design incorporates database-side access restrictions, validation mechanisms, and controlled workflow enforcement to support data isolation and secure coordination between system roles.\par
