\b 3.1 Background\b0\par
The challenge of last-mile logistics has been a subject of academic and industrial research for more than two decades. Early work by Punakivi, Yrjola, and Holmstrom (2001) showed that unattended delivery reception solutions can significantly reduce last-mile delivery costs, in some scenarios by up to 60% compared with traditional home delivery, mainly by reducing failed delivery attempts and enabling route consolidation [5]. This foundational insight supports the core logic behind the pickup-point model adopted in WASLE.\par
\par
The emergence of gig-economy delivery platforms during the 2010s demonstrated the practical feasibility of coordinating multiple stakeholders through mobile applications, connecting merchants, couriers, and customers through unified digital workflows. However, these platforms were largely designed for dense urban environments with mature mapping infrastructure and often rely on commission models or service structures that are not necessarily suitable for low-margin merchants in semi-urban or infrastructure-constrained contexts [6].\par
\par
More recent research has examined the environmental implications of last-mile delivery models. Several studies suggest that pickup points and parcel lockers can reduce transport inefficiencies and lower emissions compared with repeated home-delivery attempts, particularly when delivery routes are consolidated and consumer travel to collection points remains limited [7]. This body of research is directly relevant to the environmental rationale of the WASLE platform.\par
\par
Recent logistics research also emphasizes the importance of intelligent dispatching, routing optimization, and resource-aware assignment in improving delivery efficiency. In practical systems, assignment quality depends not only on driver availability, but also on route cost, operational constraints, and delivery capacity. This perspective is directly reflected in WASLE through its automated assignment logic, which combines business constraints with route-based decision-making.\par
\par
In the Lebanese context, parcel collection through neighborhood shops or informal local businesses is already practiced in an unstructured manner. WASLE builds on this existing social and logistical behavior by formalizing it within a digital platform that introduces traceability, coordination, payment accountability, auditable remittance workflows, and automated order-assignment support.\par
\par
\b 3.2 Local Market Solutions (Toters)\b0\par
Toters is a prominent Lebanese on-demand delivery platform offering mobile-first coordination for food, grocery, and other local store deliveries, with real-time order tracking and driver visibility features [8]. It demonstrates the viability of app-based delivery coordination in the Lebanese market. However, its operating model is primarily oriented toward on-demand consumer delivery rather than structured e-commerce parcel fulfillment. In addition, it does not provide an open coordination platform for independent delivery companies, nor does it formalize community businesses as pickup points with dedicated cash remittance workflows.\par
\par
From an operational perspective, Toters is also centered on platform-managed delivery execution rather than a configurable multi-stakeholder assignment environment. It does not expose a capacity-aware and company-constrained automated assignment model tailored to independent delivery networks.\par
\par
\b 3.3 Logistics Providers (Aramex Lebanon)\b0\par
Aramex Lebanon represents the local presence of a regional logistics provider offering parcel shipping, courier services, and shipment tracking capabilities [9]. Aramex provides a professional logistics service with established operational processes and reliable tracking infrastructure. However, its model is closer to that of a conventional logistics operator than an open digital coordination platform. It does not provide a multi-stakeholder environment in which independent delivery companies, pickup point operators, merchants, and drivers can collaborate through a shared workflow, and it does not include a localized pickup point enrollment mechanism or payment-remittance model tailored to neighborhood-scale logistics.\par
\par
Moreover, its service model does not focus on automated driver assignment under localized business constraints such as same-company matching, vehicle-capacity filtering, and lightweight route optimization within a shared platform ecosystem.\par
\par
\b 3.4 Global Pickup-Point Networks\b0\par
Mature pickup-point implementations such as DHL Packstation in Germany and Amazon Locker in the United States illustrate the scalability and user acceptance of the pickup-point model in advanced logistics markets [10], [11]. These systems demonstrate that redirecting deliveries to collection nodes can improve convenience and reduce failed home delivery attempts. However, they are generally proprietary and depend on dedicated physical infrastructure, specialized lockers, and large-scale capital investment. They also do not address the specific operational requirement of community-level cash remittance handling in a cash-dominant environment.\par
\par
WASLE adapts the same underlying concept to the Lebanese context by relying on existing community businesses as pickup points, thereby reducing infrastructure costs and making deployment more feasible in a resource-constrained environment. In addition, unlike static pickup-point networks, WASLE integrates routing-aware and capacity-aware automated assignment logic to improve operational efficiency within a distributed local delivery ecosystem.\par
\par
