The comparative analysis demonstrates that existing solutions address only isolated aspects of the WASLE problem space. None provides an integrated platform that combines multi-role coordination, open pickup-point enrollment, formalized cash remittance management, and accessibility for semi-urban environments. In addition, the automated assignment capabilities of most existing solutions are either internal, opaque, or not adapted to the operational realities of independent delivery networks.\par
\par
The main innovation of WASLE lies in the formalization of community businesses as active, digitally managed, and financially accountable logistics nodes, thereby enabling a scalable and cost-efficient alternative to traditional last-mile delivery models. This innovation is further strengthened by the integration of automated driver assignment based on company constraints, vehicle capacity, and route estimation, making the platform both operationally practical and technically intelligent.\par
